Insurance companies already use data (driving apps, health wearables) to price risk individually — the logical endpoint is that anyone with unfavourable data pays more for everything.
Facial recognition in public spaces has a false positive rate of 2–5% in real-world deployments — 'wrongly matched' individuals have been arrested, denied boarding, and publicly shamed.
Once data is collected, control over its future use is lost — corporations sell to data brokers, are acquired by other companies, or are compelled to share with governments.
